1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,857
Coach: Start a fresh excerpt from the welcome-activity discussion.

2
00:00:03,857 --> 00:00:11,571
Coach: The group still needs one activity that helps people meet and find out about the weekly drawing group,

3
00:00:11,571 --> 00:00:16,714
Coach: within twenty minutes in the main room using existing paper and pens.

4
00:00:17,064 --> 00:00:21,778
Coach: The learner begins to connect paired introductions to the drawing group.

5
00:00:21,778 --> 00:00:25,636
Coach: Before that point is finished, Noah starts another idea.

6
00:00:25,636 --> 00:00:28,636
Coach: The two lanes show this brief overlap;

7
00:00:28,636 --> 00:00:32,493
Coach: they do not tell us whether it was deliberate.

8
00:00:32,843 --> 00:00:35,343
You: After exchanging names, pairs could—

9
00:00:35,693 --> 00:00:38,264
Noah: We could each draw or write—

10
00:00:38,614 --> 00:00:43,328
Coach: We have two unfinished turns, not a complete comparison of ideas.

11
00:00:43,328 --> 00:00:48,900
Coach: A small repair can decide who continues while keeping the other point available.

12
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:52,328
Coach: It need not decide which proposal is better.

13
00:00:52,678 --> 00:00:59,107
Coach: You can offer someone room to finish, or ask briefly to finish your own point.

14
00:00:59,107 --> 00:01:02,107
Coach: The useful choice depends on the moment.

15
00:01:02,107 --> 00:01:05,535
Coach: Giving a turn away does not mean agreeing,

16
00:01:05,535 --> 00:01:09,821
Coach: and finishing a turn is not a claim to leadership.

17
00:01:10,171 --> 00:01:13,171
Coach: If you let the other person continue,

18
00:01:13,171 --> 00:01:16,600
Coach: keep a short reminder of your unfinished point.

19
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:19,600
Coach: Otherwise, an intended return can be lost.

20
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:22,171
Coach: The reminder should preserve the point

21
00:01:22,171 --> 00:01:25,600
Coach: without competing with the next speaker’s whole explanation.

22
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:28,521
Coach: Privately identify the immediate need here.

23
00:01:28,521 --> 00:01:31,521
Coach: Is it to choose a final activity,

24
00:01:31,521 --> 00:01:36,664
Coach: or to make the next turn hearable and preserve what remains unfinished?

25
00:01:36,664 --> 00:01:40,950
Coach: Keep the task decision separate from this turn-taking repair.

26
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:51,014
Coach: Compare these next turns.

27
00:01:51,014 --> 00:01:56,157
Coach: One asks to finish, one tries to settle the activity too early,

28
00:01:56,157 --> 00:02:00,871
Coach: and one makes room while keeping a point to return to.

29
00:02:00,871 --> 00:02:03,014
Coach: Examine what each actually accomplishes.

30
00:02:03,364 --> 00:02:05,935
Coach: The peer replies are written possibilities.

31
00:02:05,935 --> 00:02:09,793
Coach: A polite request still needs attention to the answer.

32
00:02:09,793 --> 00:02:13,650
Coach: Treat permission to continue as permission for a turn,

33
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:16,650
Coach: not as approval of the idea itself.

34
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,286
You: Go ahead, Noah.

35
00:02:26,286 --> 00:02:33,143
You: Afterwards, I'd like to finish my point about linking the paired introductions to the drawing group.

36
00:02:33,493 --> 00:02:38,636
Noah: We could each draw or write one thing we'd like to try,

37
00:02:38,636 --> 00:02:41,207
Noah: then use that to introduce ourselves.

38
00:02:41,557 --> 00:02:47,557
Coach: This gives Noah room to explain and keeps a visible point to return to.

39
00:02:47,557 --> 00:02:50,986
Coach: The learner still needs to make that return;

40
00:02:50,986 --> 00:02:53,986
Coach: the phrase alone does not complete it.

41
00:02:53,986 --> 00:02:57,843
Coach: Hearing the proposal is not deciding to adopt it.

42
00:02:58,193 --> 00:03:02,479
Coach: The shared principle is to make the next turn clear

43
00:03:02,479 --> 00:03:04,622
Coach: while protecting an unfinished point.

44
00:03:04,622 --> 00:03:07,622
Coach: This is a decision about speaking order.

45
00:03:07,622 --> 00:03:11,908
Coach: It does not settle whether an idea fits the task

46
00:03:11,908 --> 00:03:14,479
Coach: or whether anyone agrees with it.

47
00:03:14,829 --> 00:03:19,972
Coach: If you offer someone the turn, listen to what follows before returning.

48
00:03:19,972 --> 00:03:24,257
Coach: You can then connect your point to what was said,

49
00:03:24,257 --> 00:03:27,257
Coach: or explain why it still needs attention.

50
00:03:27,257 --> 00:03:31,543
Coach: Do not assume your parked point has already been understood.

51
00:03:31,893 --> 00:03:33,607
Coach: In a separate example,

52
00:03:33,607 --> 00:03:40,464
Coach: we can now see the learner’s complete point about linking the introductions to the drawing group.

53
00:03:40,814 --> 00:03:46,385
You: After exchanging names, pairs could identify one question about the weekly drawing group.

54
00:03:46,735 --> 00:03:49,735
Coach: That adds a possible question-forming step.

55
00:03:49,735 --> 00:03:53,164
Coach: It does not yet tell newcomers the answer

56
00:03:53,164 --> 00:03:57,021
Coach: or identify where reliable group information will come from.

57
00:03:57,021 --> 00:04:02,164
Coach: The task connection still needs development, even after the turn is clear.

58
00:04:02,514 --> 00:04:05,943
Coach: Return privately to the original two unfinished turns.

59
00:04:05,943 --> 00:04:11,943
Coach: Choose a short way to arrange the next turn and preserve the other point.

60
00:04:11,943 --> 00:04:15,371
Coach: You may ask to finish or offer room.

61
00:04:15,371 --> 00:04:17,514
Coach: Pause if you want longer.

62
00:04:25,864 --> 00:04:29,293
Coach: Check what your chosen move would leave unfinished.

63
00:04:29,293 --> 00:04:34,007
Coach: A useful repair makes space without pretending the discussion is settled.

64
00:04:34,007 --> 00:04:37,007
Coach: Next, we will consider inviting a contribution

65
00:04:37,007 --> 00:04:41,293
Coach: while leaving the person free to pass or join later.
